WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.280
Speaker 1: We have a voicemail number.

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:04.730
That number is 2192.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:11.610
David P, here is a caller making a point about
those who love to say, I've done my research.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.009
Take a listen to this.

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:14.009
Speaker 3: Hi, David.

00:00:14.009 --> 00:00:18.140
I think we really need to push back against
people who say they do their own research,

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.080
which is this idea that research is something
you can do.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.690
Research is a tool used to describe what you
do.

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.960
It's used to describe grant funded activities
costing millions of dollars with tens of thousands

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:26.960
of people involved.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.679
This idea that people are doing research is
absurd.

00:00:29.679 --> 00:00:30.679
Okay?

00:00:30.679 --> 00:00:32.160
What they're actually doing is consuming information.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.020
Are they critical of that information?

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:36.510
Are they consuming the right kinds of information?

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.300
These are the things we need to be discussing.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.190
But when it comes to discussions about should
you do your own research or whatever, it's

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:45.730
absurd because they're using this term research
inappropriately.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.390
Speaker 1: Okay, I get the idea.

00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:51.030
So what we're talking about here, this is
interesting.

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:53.870
They're talking about colloquial versus formal
language.

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.230
And I'll give you another example.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:59.680
Many people will often say in casual conversation
or either like a political analyst will say,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.770
I have a theory, or people will write to me
and say, David, I have a theory.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:05.770
The term theory.

00:01:05.770 --> 00:01:08.470
In science means it's a scientific theory,
meaning well substantiated explanation of

00:01:08.470 --> 00:01:09.840
some aspect of the world.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.320
We have vast evidence, we have observations,
we have experimentation, logical reasoning.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.420
It's a comprehensive framework that the science
community has said this is the best explanation

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.039
we have for some phenomena and it is evidence
based, etc.

00:01:20.039 --> 00:01:22.229
It's been tested, it's often been peer reviewed,
etc.

00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:23.229
It's been scrutinized.

00:01:23.229 --> 00:01:24.579
In casual conversation, when we say have a
theory.

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:26.850
We know that what you mean is you have a hypothesis.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.540
And yeah, sure, to some degree.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.729
Would it be more accurate for people to say,
I have a hypothesis about what's going to

00:01:30.729 --> 00:01:31.729
happen in 2024?

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:34.390
Sure, it's more accurate, but sometimes we
use the same words differently when it's colloquial

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:35.390
versus when it's formal.

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:36.390
When we talk about research.

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.310
It's true when someone says it, when some
anti-vax person says, I did my research or

00:01:39.310 --> 00:01:41.860
when I say it, or whatever the case may be,
we're talking about someone who is gathering

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:42.860
information.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.500
It's it's an it's a information gathering
process with which you then are ideally coming

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.040
to some conclusion about phenomena in everyday
life.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:49.229
Do vaccines cause autism?

00:01:49.229 --> 00:01:50.820
Well, I've researched it.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:51.820
Okay.

00:01:51.820 --> 00:02:02.479
But you mean as you gathered information that
others have done other research other people

00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:11.520
have done true actual research would be we
are comprehensively and systematically using

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:21.280
established methodologies to generate new
knowledge, and then they are being peer reviewed

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.280
and.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.030
Okay, I get it.

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:36.040
Research is a rigorous methodology that most
of us are not doing when we say we're doing

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.260
our research.

00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:40.150
However, I would focus more on the people
who say I've done my research.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.470
Often what they mean is they read a FoxNews.com
article.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:43.470
We would do better.

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:47.830
I believe, instead of saying random people
don't do research, you're not working in an

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:50.560
academic research setting or a medical research
setting.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:51.569
Instead of focusing on that.

00:02:51.569 --> 00:02:54.409
I would focus on giving people a better structure
for getting information about topics they're

00:02:54.409 --> 00:02:56.650
trying to learn about so they don't end up
on conspiracy websites.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:01.560
I think we would get more from that, but obviously
the voice mail is accurate in that the term

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:11.110
research means something that is very different
than, you know, sitting at home and looking

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:12.110
at a Daily Wire article.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.209
I agree with that 100%.

